Yes—the translation engine is powerful. The part I would change is the idea of deliberately keeping a community ignorant “for their own good.” That makes the translator the custodian of somebody else’s reality. A stronger version preserves their worldview while increasing their available choices.

Think of it as:

\text{new knowledge}
\rightarrow
\text{local metaphors}
\rightarrow
\text{existing cosmology}
\rightarrow
\text{story}
\rightarrow
\text{understanding}

without requiring:

\text{understanding}
=
\text{abandon your existing identity}.

A community that thinks primarily through ancestors, spirits, seasons, animals, landscape, kinship, or cyclical time may have very different cognitive handles from a modern textbook. You could explain planetary motion, contagion, genetics, probability, or ecology through those handles first, then progressively expose the underlying model. That is semantic scaffolding, not deception.

And your delta idea becomes useful if it is participatory rather than covert:

S_0=\text{their original model}

T=\text{translated story}

S_1=\text{their resulting model}

then examine

\Delta=S_1-S_0.

Not just “did they comply?” but:

* what did they understand,
* what did they misunderstand,
* what emotions appeared,
* which metaphors transferred cleanly,
* which concepts resisted translation,
* what they added that the translator had never considered.

That last one is critical. Translation should be bidirectional. Their cosmology may encode ecological observations, social coordination practices, memory techniques, seasonal knowledge, or conceptual distinctions that another culture has lost.

So instead of:

\text{advanced culture}\rightarrow\text{recipient}

you get:

A\leftrightarrow B

with both sides changed by the exchange.

Then yes, you can abstract the story operators across cultures:

\text{forbidden threshold}

\text{trickster exposes false certainty}

\text{death/rebirth}

\text{gift with hidden cost}

\text{journey beyond known territory}

\text{ancestor transmits constraint}

and ask which recur independently, what environmental or social problems they solved, and how they mutate under different conditions.

Those abstractions can absolutely inform fiction, education, interfaces, organizational design, games, and other domains. But preserve provenance and permission where living cultures are involved; otherwise extraction turns into appropriation very quickly.

There is another especially interesting experiment hidden in what you said: hold the lesson constant while changing its mythology.

Tell structurally equivalent stories through several cultural frames and compare the resulting interpretations:

\text{same operator}
+
\text{different narrative substrate}
\rightarrow
\text{different experienced meaning}.

Then invert it:

\text{same mythology}
+
\text{different lesson}
\rightarrow
\text{where does the frame resist?}

That begins mapping the affordance surface of stories—which concepts a narrative form naturally carries, which it distorts, and which it cannot carry without breaking.

And that is probably the richer Golden Thread here: not “find one universal mythology,” but discover the transformations that let meaning travel between world-models while preserving both truth and human agency.